hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well the psychologist  Lawrence Kohlberg in his theory of moral  development distinguishes six stages  through which human beings can pass and  we all begin at stage one where we're  oriented towards essentially punishment  reward and authority and we have the  capacity to develop all the way up to  the highest level where we are concerned  with universal ethical principles with  conscience with these applying these  things in a non conventional a post  conventional as the word that he uses  for that manner and how do we actually  progress along those stages that's the  question that he's interested in one of  the sub questions is whether our  educational institutions are well suited  for promoting moral development in the  way that they set up education and  Kohlberg thought that quite a few of the  schools of his own era were not well  suited in the way that they were  organized in the way that they were  thought-out in the activities that  student that students were encountering  the messages that they were getting from  teachers administrators and also other  people as well was  promoting moral development in a  particularly adequate way and he set out  why he thought this was the case so one  is an emphasis upon Authority which  interestingly he diagnosed is going sort  of hand in hand with a unwillingness to  engage in education that explicitly took  stands on moral issues there was sort of  a perception at the time and I think  this this still runs throughout many  school settings that it's really the  family or religious institutions or  something else that should be teaching  these moral truths to children as he put  it the school should be value neutral  and Kohlberg thinks that that's  completely off-base it's nonsense to  ever think that you're value neutral you  are by taking the stance of value  neutrality actually saying something  quite profound about values if you strip  away any sort of substantive moral  teaching you still have to maintain some  sort of order or else you can't have an  institution at all there's a lot that  has to go into organizing things and  disciplining and structuring if you want  to educate children all you need to do  is spend a little bit of time with them  in an educational institution and you'll  you'll see exactly what we mean  so his diagnosis is that whether they  are deliberately doing things this way  or not there often tends to be this  emphasis upon Authority and not on  something that would go beyond that what  he calls justice and orientation towards  justice and so he says that very often  the schools he says the school  atmosphere is generally a blend of stage  one the punishment morality do what you  need to do so you don't get punished  whatever gets you punished is bad or  whatever it doesn't get you punished is  good and stage four the law and or  view which if a person had progressed to  that level they're able to recognize  authority and an order as something good  in their own right but unfortunately  this isn't where most children are and  so he says that this often fails to  impress or stimulate children who are  involved in their own stage 2 or stage 3  moral philosophies which is probably the  bulk of of most children and so schools  end up doing a fairly poor job in  promoting moral development if it  happens it usually happens despite what  is going on in the classroom the  gymnasium and other school settings  Kohlberg thinks that schools really need  to as he says take moral questions  seriously that it should be an integral  part of Education and not just look for  or promote the right answers and there's  many ways of doing this I should mention  that you know whatever side of the  political spectrum you're on there's a  tendency to think that the other side in  education is carrying out indoctrination  in the right and we're putting right in  quote marks right to show that we're  being ironic about it right answers but  really whatever side of the spectrum  you're coming from educational  institutions will have a set of right  answers that they're promoting whether  they're progressive or conservative  there'll be some right answers and  Kohlberg says you know it's good to have  answers quite often it's good to have  them so you can push off against them  but just being told the answers just  memorizing things just going through  what used to be in the old days called  catechesis is not really promoting any  moral development and you see this over  and over again in elementary schools all  the way up to high schools even in some  colleges where students are being given  what the right answers are and expected  to memorize them maybe even give it a  little bit of explanation about it but  never going through the intellectual and  effective and practical work of trying  those answers on  and finding where they don't entirely  fit and then reworking them for  themselves the other thing that he  criticizes schools for and again this  goes back to the question of value  neutrality is that instead of using  concepts like moral education  he says they use terms like  socialization or acculturation or  citizenship and these have been used to  refer to the moral impact on students  what is his problem with that he says  these terms ignore the problem of the  standard or principle of value implied  by such terms why should you be  socialized you know we often hear the  argument that you know it's very  important that children go to public  school so that they'll be properly  socialized well sometimes they are  socialized in horrible ways if they just  simply encounter bullying that's never  punished or addressed what lessons are  they actually learning socialization is  not by itself automatically a good thing  and we have to be attentive to what's  being implied by these terms or  citizenship in the places that still  actually have some attention to that  unfortunately civics often falls out of  the curriculum as things get pinched and  squeezed we could say the same thing  about all the other aspects of life that  that often don't get their way into  curricula Kohlberg thinks that you know  instead of just focusing on these ideas  like socialization or citizenship we  should just come straight out and say  the school like the family like the  workplace like every other institution  within modern society is a place where  moral education should be taking place  and if it's not taking place it's going  to take place but it's going to take  place poorly the wrong modes of moral  education are going to take the place of  things that would be more productive so  what does he propose instead he says  that the aim of schools  other things should be as he puts it  personal development towards more  complex ways of reasoning and this is  embodied in those six stages that he has  or levels level one is very primitive  moral reasoning and then again that is  where we all begin as children  punishment and a reward but eventually  we can move past that to the second  level we can start to value other  people's desires much more  instrumentally we can start carrying out  more complex practical reasoning we can  start explaining ourselves in terms that  refer to a wider moral universe level 3  where we start to actually care about  what other people think and feel and how  they're being affected and whether they  like us  that's an advance that is a stage that  many people never actually attain but  just learn to fake but to actually  attain it is some sort of achievement  it's a moral development and so Kohlberg  thinks that schools ought to be  promoting this all the way up to the  highest level he calls this a  qualitative transformation of the form  in the form of the child's thought or  action and what does that mean  it means that the child as they're  making choices as they're engaging in  action so in behavioral context as they  are thinking things out and perhaps even  communicating those ideas to other  people there is a transformation in  quality not just in quantity they're not  just giving longer explanations they're  better explanations of what it is that  they think is right or wrong they're  more adequate so then the question is  well how do you actually bring this  about  what are his recommendations for  educational institutions and I think if  we were going to apply these we would  have to do it in sort of a triage way  we'd have to see what we can actually do  at first and how many resources of  school  has before we try to do a  one-size-fits-all model so the first  thing that he says is we really ought to  use justice as an organizing principle  for moral education what would that mean  it would mean going beyond just sort of  you might say pragmatic conceptions of  keeping everybody quiet or producing as  many well-educated people as possible by  meeting certain you know standards when  it comes to content or you know imposing  Authority or anything like that we would  in fact need people involved in the  process who were at higher levels of  moral development and who could embody  and make decisions according to those  sorts of reasoning we probably would  need level five and six people rather  than just level two through four which  may require developing a certain talent  pool among educators and administrators  that would be a important project to  carry out but using Kohlberg's  conception of justice moving through  these stages and thinking about the sort  of justifications that are given for  moral reasoning and decision-making that  would be an integral part of this  another is as the students move from  stage to stage he says well when does  this actually happen it doesn't happen  because you give them a class or some  videos or you know a pamphlet about how  to do stage 3 reasoning or anything like  that for those who are already at States  through reasoning that could be helpful  for those who are at the cusp of two and  three that could be useful as well but  it really depends on creating  opportunities for the students to move  from one level to the next and he says  well why do people move from one level  to the next it's because they come to  realize that the perspective that they  have at the current stage is not really  adequate there's something  defficient about it and you you can't  simply tell somebody that that's the  case they have to be able to see that  realize it and then want something more  or else they're never going to progress  so you can't make a whole school yard  full of angels by giving them class  after class after class or workshop  after workshop or doing with anything  like that by just telling them the way  that they're supposed to behave they  have to engage in enough other  activities that will get them to the  point where they can see that maybe they  should shift things maybe they should  take on a better perspective then you  can give them that better perspective  now the other thing that he talks about  is the absolute central importance of  social interactions and of what he says  taking other's roles that means engaging  in some sort of discussion where you're  hearing other people's perspectives on  moral reasoning and then thinking  through conflicts you cannot advance in  Kohlberg's view and within his his  system you could say without having  conflicts that you have to address now  these could be real-life conflicts a  fight on the school you know playground  or it could be dilemmas that the school  itself is facing you know where should  we spend the money you know a perennial  great example of this are we spending  too much money on athletics and not  enough on all these other important  activities within the school that can be  opened up for a public discussion one  that I think many principals would be  unwilling to have but that's because  they are not embodying what Kohlberg is  suggesting here it actually be very good  for students we have to think through  conflicts and we have to have the  opportunity to screw up we have to have  the opportunity to provide inadequate  justifications for our points of view  and then have other people say no no I  think that you're missing something here  and then we can have the possibility of  moving towards something better  he thinks that schools should be set up  less to give all the right answers and  more to promote thinking now it's not to  be a sort of content less open-ended how  do we all feel let's make a drum circle  and share all of our ideas and nobody's  right and nobody's wrong instead he  thinks no we need to help students  actually consider the inconsistencies  and inadequacies in their moral  frameworks and in those of others within  whom they may see themselves so there is  definitely some right and wrong being  used here but that's not the ending of  the discussion rather it's part of the  discussion and so Kohlberg here I've  been drawing on this moral development  to review of the theory piece but he  talks about this in many other places is  sketching out for us what genuine moral  education might look like as opposed to  the unfortunate fact similes or absences  of it that we often tend to provide to  our students  you
 

